  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Michigan totaled $5.1B in FY2026 to date. Procurement activity produced 141,398 awards with an average award size of $35.7K, and obligations were down 23.6% year over year. Looking back by fiscal year, obligations were $6.8B in FY21, $9B in FY22, $8.4B in FY23, $8B in FY24, and $6.6B in FY25; the current FY26 total is $5.1B. The Department of Defense and the General Services Administration were the largest buyers, at $2.5B and $1.8B respectively. Michigan’s top vendors reflect the state’s industrial base: GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. received $1.3B, while Ford, General Motors, and FCA received $631M, $607M, and $491M respectively. Industry concentrations are concentrated in vehicle and defense manufacturing with Automobile Manufacturing at $1.7B and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing at $1.3B. Other notable industry buckets include Other Engine Equipment Manufacturing at $148M and Petroleum Refineries at $131M. These figures are fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations rather than completed expenditures.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What did federal contracting look like in Michigan this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Michigan recorded $5.1B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date, spread across 141,398 awards with an average award size of $35.7K. Obligations were down 23.6% year over year. Annual totals show a peak at $9B in FY22 and a decline to $6.6B in FY25 before the FY26 to date total of $5.1B. The pattern points to a pullback from the FY22 high, with the current year still partial."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominated obligations in Michigan?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounted for $2.5B of obligations and the General Services Administration accounted for $1.8B in FY2026 to date. Other significant buyers included the Department of Homeland Security at $185M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $176M. On the supplier side, GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. received $1.3B, Ford Motor Co received $631M, and General Motors LLC received $607M. Industry concentration was highest in Automobile Manufacturing at $1.7B and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing at $1.3B."
      }
